*Table 3. Income statement summary. Growth for FY2024 uses FY2023 as the base.*

Revenue growth picked up to 17.8% in FY2026 from 14.9% a year earlier. That is a lot for a company already past $300 billion in sales.

The margin story is more interesting than the growth. Gross margin slipped from 69.8% to 67.9% over two years because the cost of running cloud and AI services is rising faster than the revenue they bring in (cost of service and other revenue grew about 27% in FY2026). Even so, operating margin rose to 46.8%, because operating expenses grew only about 7%, and research spending fell from 12.0% to 10.7% of revenue. The fourth quarter also carried costs from a voluntary retirement programme and impairment charges on Xbox, which Microsoft called out itself.

Net income rose 31%, well ahead of the 21% growth in operating income, and I wanted to understand the gap. It comes from "other income", which swung from a loss of $4.9 billion in FY2025 to a gain of $10.7 billion in FY2026, mostly from investments. Microsoft shows the effect of its OpenAI stake separately: stripping it out, net income is $128.8 billion (up 22%) and diluted EPS is $17.28 rather than $17.95. The fourth quarter also included a $3.2 billion gain on an investment in Anthropic. So the operating business grew around 21% to 22%, and the extra growth in reported profit is investment gains that may not repeat.

Two smaller points. The effective tax rate was 19.4% in FY2026, up from 17.6% in FY2025 and 18.2% in FY2024, which took a little off earnings growth. And diluted shares were almost flat (7,453 million against 7,465 million), so EPS growth is coming from real profit, not from buybacks shrinking the share count.

