*Table 4. Selected balance sheet items. Source: Microsoft Form 8-K, 29 July 2026.*

The headline is property and equipment, up about $108 billion in a single year. That is the data centre build-out showing up on the balance sheet. Equity grew faster than liabilities because Microsoft kept most of its profit (retained earnings rose by about $90.5 billion), and the equity and other investments line more than doubled, which fits the OpenAI and Anthropic positions.

Liquidity is still comfortable, though a bit thinner. The current ratio fell from 1.35 to 1.23, helped down by a jump in accounts payable, which is what you would expect when suppliers are building you data centres. Debt is small. Total debt is $40.3 billion against $76.8 billion of cash and short-term investments, so Microsoft is in a net cash position of about $36.5 billion, and debt to equity is only 0.09.

One thing I want to flag. "Other long-term liabilities" rose by about $20 billion to $65.1 billion. I believe this includes lease obligations for data centres, but the earnings release does not break it out, so I cannot confirm it. If it is right, the real commitments Microsoft has taken on are bigger than the debt line suggests. I come back to this in the limitations.

