*Table 5. Cash flow summary. Sources: Form 8-K (FY2025, FY2026) and Form 10-K (FY2024).*

Cash generation from the business itself is excellent. Operating cash flow rose 34% to $182.9 billion, which is 1.37 times net income, so the profit is real cash and not accounting. The problem sits one line lower. Capital spending jumped 80% to $115.9 billion, about 35% of revenue compared with 18% two years ago.

The result is that free cash flow fell 6.5% to $67.0 billion in a year when net income grew 31%. Free cash flow as a share of net income has gone from 0.84 to 0.70 to 0.50. If I had to pick one finding from this whole exercise, this is it. Microsoft is more profitable than ever on paper, but each extra dollar of profit now leaves less cash behind because so much is being poured into capacity. It is also still paying out about 73% of free cash flow through dividends and buybacks. That is fine while operating cash flow keeps growing, but it leaves less room if growth slows.

Depreciation is the follow-on effect. It rose from $29.4 billion to $38.5 billion (about 31%) and will keep climbing as the new data centres come into service, which is why I let the margin drift down in the forecast later.

