A quick DuPont check helps explain return on equity. For FY2026, ROE of 34.0% equals a net margin of 40.3%, multiplied by asset turnover of 0.48, multiplied by an equity multiplier of 1.75. Profit margin is doing nearly all the work. Turnover is low and leverage is modest, so the return does not rely on borrowing.

## 8. Valuation Model

### 8.1 Why I used a discounted cash flow

I used a discounted cash flow (DCF) model because it forces me to write down what I believe about growth, margins, spending and risk, and then shows what those beliefs are worth. I did not build a comparable-company table. I could not verify current peer figures to the standard I wanted, and I would rather leave it out than fill it with numbers I cannot stand behind. Instead I cross-check the DCF with Microsoft's own multiples in Section 8.7.

### 8.2 Discount rate

Discount rates are a hard topic this year. The Federal Reserve raised its target range to 3.75% to 4.00% on 16 September 2026, its first increase since 2023, and the 10-year Treasury yield moved to about 5%, the highest in roughly two decades. That pushes up the risk-free rate, and with it the required return on any stock.

| Input | Value | Basis |
| --- | ---: | --- |
| Risk-free rate | 5.0% | 10-year Treasury yield, about 5% in mid-September 2026 |
| Equity beta | 1.10 | Yahoo Finance, 5-year monthly |
| Equity risk premium | 4.5% | My assumption, within the usual 4% to 6% range |
| Cost of equity | 9.95% | Risk-free rate + beta x equity risk premium |
| Pre-tax cost of debt | 5.3% | My assumption, slightly above Treasuries for a top-rated borrower |
| Tax rate | 19% | Close to the FY2026 effective rate of 19.4% |
| Weight of debt / equity | 1.0% / 99.0% | Debt of $40.3B against market value of about $3.9 trillion |
| WACC | 9.9% | Used as the base discount rate |

*Table 7. Discount rate build-up.*

Because debt is about 1% of total capital, the WACC is almost the same as the cost of equity. The number is sensitive to my risk-free rate and equity risk premium, so I show a sensitivity table later rather than pretending there is one right answer.

### 8.3 Forecast assumptions

The forecast runs ten years, FY2027 to FY2036, because a company growing in the mid-teens cannot sensibly be put on a steady growth rate after only five years. Growth fades gradually, then a terminal growth rate takes over. I calculate free cash flow to the firm as operating profit after tax, plus depreciation, minus capital spending. I do not add back stock-based compensation, so it is treated as a real cost. That is more conservative than the free cash flow Microsoft reports.

| Assumption | Base case | Reasoning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Revenue growth | 16% in FY2027, fading to 5% by FY2036 | Microsoft's guidance for the first quarter of FY2027 implies growth of about 16%. The fade reflects the law of large numbers. |
| Operating margin | 45% throughout | Below the FY2026 level of 46.8% because depreciation will keep rising. |
| Tax rate | 19% | In line with recent history. |
| Depreciation and amortisation | 13% of revenue rising to 16%, then 15% | FY2026 was 11.6%. It rises as the new assets are put to use. |
| Capital spending (all-in) | 38% of revenue in FY2027, falling to 18% by FY2036 | FY2026 was 34.9% on cash capex alone. I set it higher because some data centre capacity is funded through finance leases, which this line does not capture. |
| Terminal growth | 3.5% | Roughly long-run nominal growth of the economy. |
| Working capital | No net change | Microsoft collects cash from customers before it delivers service, so working capital is not a drag. I left it out for simplicity. |

