Look at the first forecast year. Free cash flow to the firm is only $44 billion in FY2027, below the $67 billion Microsoft produced in FY2026, because spending rises faster than profit. It then climbs as capital spending flattens against growing revenue. Most of the value in the model comes from the later years.

### 8.4 Results by scenario

Discounting the base case at 9.9% gives an enterprise value of about $2.95 trillion. Adding cash and short-term investments ($76.8 billion) and equity investments ($36.3 billion), and subtracting debt ($40.3 billion), gives an equity value of about $3.02 trillion. Dividing by 7,443 million diluted shares gives **$406 per share**. About 66% of that enterprise value sits in the terminal value, which is typical for a growth company and a reminder to treat the answer as a range.

| Scenario | Revenue growth | Operating margin | Capex % of revenue | Terminal growth | Value per share | Versus $529.05 |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Bear | 13% fading to 4.0% | 42% | 40% to 20.0% | 3.0% | $257 | -51% |
| Base | 16% fading to 5.0% | 45% | 38% to 18.0% | 3.5% | $406 | -23% |
| Bull | 18% fading to 6.0% | 47% | 36% to 16.5% | 4.0% | $538 | +2% |
| Weighted (25/50/25) |  |  |  |  | $402 | -24% |

*Table 10. Scenario results at a WACC of 9.9%.*

The bear case assumes growth slows faster, margins fall to 42% and spending stays heavy. The bull case assumes growth holds up longer, margins reach 47% and capital spending comes down sooner. The 25/50/25 weights are my own judgement.

